  <Slide
    id="eight-fallacies"
    eyebrow="04 · DESIGN CHALLENGES"
    title="The 8 Fallacies of Distributed Computing (Peter Deutsch)"
    layout="table"
    unit="Unit 01: Introduction"
  >
    <div class="my-auto">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 font-medium mb-3">
        Engineers transitioning from uniprocessor software to distributed systems frequently assume false invariants that lead to catastrophic production failures:
      </p>

      <Table
        headers={["Fallacy (False Assumption)", "Physical Reality in Real Networks", "Engineering Countermeasure"]}
        rows={[
          ["1. The network is reliable", "Packet loss, dropped frames, switch reboots occur constantly", "Idempotent retries, message acknowledgments"],
          ["2. Latency is zero", "Speed of light limits WAN latency to 50–200ms", "Asynchronous non-blocking I/O, edge caching"],
          ["3. Bandwidth is infinite", "Network throughput saturates under high concurrent load", "Binary serialization (Protobuf), data compression"],
          ["4. The network is secure", "Packets traverse untrusted public routing nodes", "End-to-end TLS encryption, mTLS certificates"],
          ["5. Topology does not change", "Servers auto-scale, fail, and migrate dynamically", "Dynamic service discovery (Consul, etcd)"],
          ["6. There is one administrator", "Multi-tenant clouds cross international corporate boundaries", "Federated identity (OAuth/SAML), zero-trust IAM"],
          ["7. Transport cost is zero", "Data egress fees and serialization CPU costs are significant", "Minimize cross-region egress data transfer"],
          ["8. The network is homogeneous", "Clients use diverse mobile devices, OS kernels, and MTUs", "Strict JSON/Protobuf schemas, standard protocols"]
        ]}
      />
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Walk through Fallacies 1 and 2 in detail. Emphasize that assuming zero latency or 100% reliability leads to blocking thread deadlocks.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 15: CORE DESIGN ISSUE - HETEROGENEITY
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="issue-heterogeneity"
    eyebrow="04 · DESIGN CHALLENGES"
    title="Design Challenge 1: Heterogeneity & Data Marshalling"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="Unit 01: Introduction"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Distributed systems encompass hardware from different manufacturers (x86, ARM, RISC-V), distinct operating systems (Linux, Windows, macOS, iOS), multiple programming languages (Java, C++, Rust, Python, Go), and varying network protocols.
        </p>

        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300 space-y-2">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-950 uppercase">Two Architectural Solutions:</h4>
          <div class="space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-900 font-medium">
            <p class="m-0">
              <strong>1. Data Marshalling & Canonical Intermediate Representations</strong>: Data structures in local RAM are serialized into canonical, architecture-neutral wire formats (JSON, XML, or binary formats like Protocol Buffers and Apache Avro). Numbers are normalized to standard byte order (Network Byte Order = Big Endian).
            </p>
            <p class="m-0">
              <strong>2. Language-Independent Interface Definition Languages (IDLs)</strong>: Public APIs are declared in a neutral IDL (e.g. gRPC <code>.proto</code> files), from which client stubs and server skeletons are automatically compiled into target languages.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Marshalling Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "Marshalling",
              definition: "Packaging in-memory data structures into a contiguous byte stream suitable for network transmission."
            },
            {
              term: "Unmarshalling",
              definition: "Reconstructing local typed in-memory data structures from an incoming serialized network byte stream."
            },
            {
              term: "Endianness",
              definition: "Byte ordering in memory: Big-Endian (MSB first) vs Little-Endian (LSB first)."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain why passing raw C struct pointers across a network fails: different padding, endianness, and memory addresses between client and server.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 16: CORE DESIGN ISSUE - SCALABILITY & BOTTLENECKS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="issue-scalability"
    eyebrow="04 · DESIGN CHALLENGES"
    title="Design Challenge 2: Scalability Bottlenecks & Decentralized Solutions"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="Unit 01: Introduction"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="Centralized Services" badge="Bottleneck 1" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">A single centralized server handling all user registrations.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
          CPU and network bandwidth saturate as client count passes millions. <em>Solution:</em> Partition the service into decentralized autonomous shards using Consistent Hashing.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Centralized Data" badge="Bottleneck 2" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">A single central telephone book or global DNS registry file.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
          Disk I/O bandwidth and single storage limits throttle performance. <em>Solution:</em> Hierarchical namespace partitioning (DNS tree) and distributed hash tables (DHTs).
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Centralized Algorithms" badge="Bottleneck 3" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Routing algorithms requiring complete global knowledge of all nodes.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
          Collecting global state causes massive message overhead $O(N^2)$. <em>Solution:</em> Decentralized localized algorithms where nodes communicate only with immediate neighbors.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Highlight why decentralized algorithms are mandatory: no single machine can have complete state information in a system with 10 million nodes.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 17: CORE DESIGN ISSUE - FAULT TOLERANCE & RECOVERY
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="issue-fault-tolerance"
    eyebrow="04 · DESIGN CHALLENGES"
    title="Design Challenge 3: Fault Tolerance, Detection & Recovery Models"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="Unit 01: Introduction"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          A distributed system must maintain availability in the presence of partial failures. A system is said to be <strong>fault tolerant</strong> if it can continue operating correctly in the presence of hardware crashes, software bugs, and network partitions.
        </p>

        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300 space-y-2">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-950 uppercase">Four-Stage Fault Handling Pipeline:</h4>
          <ol class="text-xs text-slate-900 space-y-1.5 pl-4 list-decimal font-medium">
            <li><strong>Fault Detection</strong>: Periodic heartbeat pinging or lease timeouts to identify unresponsive nodes.</li>
            <li><strong>Fault Masking</strong>: Replicating state across $2f+1$ nodes so that $f$ simultaneous crashes can be masked without data loss.</li>
            <li><strong>Fault Containment</strong>: Isolating failed nodes using circuit breakers to prevent cascading cascade failures across the cluster.</li>
            <li><strong>Recovery</strong>: <strong>Backward Recovery</strong> (restoring system to a prior consistent checkpoint state via Write-Ahead Logs) vs. <strong>Forward Recovery</strong> (bringing system forward to a valid new state via error-correcting codes).</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Recovery Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "Fault Masking",
              definition: "Hiding component failure from the client through automatic replication and failover."
            },
            {
              term: "Backward Recovery",
              definition: "Rolling back failed transactions to a recorded previous consistent checkpoint."
            },
            {
              term: "Cascading Failure",
              definition: "A failure where one server crash shifts traffic to others, causing sequential overload crashes."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain why backward recovery requires Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) and how circuit breakers prevent cascading failures.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

