    layout="card-grid"
    unit="Unit 01: Introduction"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-auto">
      <Card title="Public Cloud Model" badge="Multi-Tenant" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Owned and operated by third-party Cloud Service Providers (CSPs).</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-3">
          Infrastructure is shared across multiple tenant organizations with logical software isolation. Provides massive economies of scale, near-infinite capacity elasticity, zero hardware maintenance, and utility pricing.
        </p>
        <div class="p-2.5 rounded bg-slate-100 border border-slate-300 text-xs text-slate-800 font-semibold">
          Trade-offs: Data resides on multi-tenant physical hardware; strict compliance auditing can be complex.
        </div>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Private Cloud Model" badge="Single-Tenant" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Dedicated infrastructure provisioned for exclusive use by one entity.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-3">
          May be owned, managed, and operated by the organization or a third party, and may exist on or off premises. Delivers maximum control over data sovereignty, physical security, custom hardware accelerators, and regulatory compliance.
        </p>
        <div class="p-2.5 rounded bg-slate-100 border border-slate-300 text-xs text-slate-800 font-semibold">
          Trade-offs: High upfront capital expenditure (CAPEX), manual capacity management, and ongoing maintenance.
        </div>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Contrast public vs private cloud. Explain why banks and defense agencies often run private clouds using OpenStack or VMware Cloud Foundation.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

