  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 31: CLOUD COMPUTING LIMITATIONS & RISKS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="cloud-limitations-depth"
    eyebrow="07 · TRADEOFF ANALYSIS"
    title="Cloud Computing: Architectural Limitations & Operational Risks"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="Unit 01: Introduction"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="Network Latency Dependency" badge="Physics" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Complete reliance on external WAN internet connectivity.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Cloud applications cannot execute if local internet connectivity fails. Real-time industrial automation (robotics, factory sensors) cannot tolerate unpredictable WAN jitter and requires local edge computing.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Vendor Lock-in & Portability" badge="Ecosystem" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Proprietary APIs and high egress data fees.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Building on proprietary services (AWS DynamoDB, Google BigQuery) ties software to that specific provider. Egress network transfer fees make exporting terabytes of data back on-premises economically prohibitive.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Multi-Tenant Interference" badge="Performance" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">The 'Noisy Neighbor' phenomenon on shared hypervisors.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Another tenant on the same physical host running heavy I/O workloads can saturate CPU L3 cache or NVMe bus bandwidth, causing temporary tail-latency latency spikes for neighboring VMs.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Discuss the Noisy Neighbor problem in multi-tenant clouds and how cloud providers use CPU pinning and hypervisor rate limiters to mitigate it.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

